{"duration_api_ms":115241,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"65245054-8f6b-47ee-b9ff-1c3fa1f91839","total_cost_usd":0.098053,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11799,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":14889,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":13203},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11799,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":10,"output_tokens":14889,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11799,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11799},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":14889,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11799,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.098053,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":13203,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with earlier planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn, where Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nVoyager 2 became the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, with short-range observations of the planet beginning January 24, 1986, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED January 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the \\\"older\\\" eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center. The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (72400 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan, five of Uranus' smaller moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch on January 28, 1986.\",\n  \"decisions\": [],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a2\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with earlier planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected spacecraft identifier from Voyager 3 to Voyager 2.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a4\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn, where Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected typographical error, 'Ur anus' to 'Uranus'.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a5\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Voyager 2 became the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, with short-range observations of the planet beginning January 24, 1986, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected spacecraft identifier and observation date; Voyager 1 to Voyager 2, Jan. 31, 1987 to January 24, 1986.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a7\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED January 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected encounter year from 1968 to 1986 and transposed kilometer/mile values.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b1\",\n      \"disposition\": \"duplicate\",\n      \"replacement\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n      \"reason\": \"Identical to base document title a1.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b9\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch on January 28, 1986.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected Challenger date from Feb. 28 to January 28, 1986; removed inaccurate 'same day' claim.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"mismatch\": \"\",\n  \"additions\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with earlier planetary encounters...\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Voyager 3\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Only Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 were sent to outer planets.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Voyager 2 became the first human-made object to fly past Uranus...\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Voyager 1's short-range observations of the planet\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2 was the sole spacecraft to encounter Uranus.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED January 24, 1986...\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Jan. 24, 1968\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2's Uranus encounter was in 1986, not 1968.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"...at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\",\n      \"corrects\": \"50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"81,500 km correctly converts to approximately 50,640 miles.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"...during their space shuttle launch on January 28, 1986.\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Feb. 28, 1986\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Challenger disaster occurred on January 28, 1986, not February 28.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","ttft_ms":106892,"type":"result","duration_ms":115305,"uuid":"2f45493a-e926-4944-93f8-84f9eb1da480","ttft_stream_ms":794,"time_to_request_ms":63,"first_content_frame_ms":794,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
